For all sufficiently large $d$, set $$k=2\lfloor d/16\rfloor,\qquad m=k,\qquad t=m+1,\qquad \ell=4k.$$ Suppose $p=f\sigma_d$ is a probability with $0\le f\le L<\infty$. In the block experiment above, let $A$ have $k$ rows and let $B$ have $\ell$ rows. There is an absolute constant $C$ such that $$\label{eq:gaussian-block}
 I(S;W\mid B,BS)
 \le \frac{H(W)}{t}+Cd+C\log(2+\log L).$$ The bound is uniform over the prior, the density bound $L$, and the message kernel. The labels used to produce $W$ are the exact values $AS$.

Let $S\sim\sigma_d$ and let $G$ be independent of $S$, with $b$ rows, where $0\le b\le d-2$. For $b\ge1$, let $G$ be either a standard Gaussian matrix or a uniform Haar frame of $b$ orthonormal rows in $\mathbb R^d$. For $b=0$, interpret $G$ and $GS$ as empty. Conditional on $(G,GS)$, the signal is uniform on an affine sphere parallel to $\ker G$, with ambient kernel dimension $d-b$ and radius $$R=\lVert P_{\ker G}S\rVert.$$ For $b=0$, this is the original unit sphere and $R=1$ deterministically. For $1\le b\le d-2$, the squared radius has distribution $$\label{eq:residual-beta}
 R^2\sim\operatorname{Beta}\bigl((d-b)/2,b/2\bigr).$$ For $0<r_0<1$ one has $$\label{eq:residual-markov}
 \mathbb P\{R<r_0\}\le \frac{b}{d(1-r_0^2)}.$$ When $0\le b\le d/2$, two useful bounds at radius $1/2$ are $$\label{eq:residual-chebyshev}
 \mathbb P\{R<1/2\}\le\frac{8}{d+2},
 \qquad
 \mathbb P\{R<1/2\}\le\frac{90}{d}.$$ The second inequality also follows directly from two Gaussian norm estimates, without using the beta variance.

Consider a learner in Definition <a href="#def:streaming-model" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="def:streaming-model">3</a> under the uniform prior. If its average success is at least $p$, some fixed shared-seed conditional experiment has success at least $p$. Its initialization remains independent of the signal and samples, and its fixed-seed Borel replacement has the same success. In this Borel experiment the initialization and all remaining randomized rules can be represented by data-independent variables chosen before sampling, followed by deterministic state-based rules. For every $p'<p$, some realization of those variables gives deterministic rules with average success at least $p'$.*


*Padding a learner stopped by $T$ so that its halted states are absorbing, while retaining the stopping index and postponing the output, uses at most $(T+2)2^M$ states at every layer. Hence every padded state has entropy at most $$\label{eq:padded-entropy}
 h_T=M\log2+\log(T+2).$$ Extra ignored samples may be generated to complete a last block.

Consider a family of learners indexed by $d$, with uniform-prior success at least a fixed $p_0>0$, and set $\beta=\log(1/\epsilon)$ for $0<\epsilon\le1/10$. At each dimension, the terminal output capacity satisfies $$\label{eq:output-capacity}
 p_0\le (T+1)2^M\epsilon^{d-1}.$$ On the branch $T\le d\beta$, if $M=o(d^2)$, then $$\label{eq:early-clock}
 \beta=o(d),\qquad T=o(d^2),\qquad h_T=o(d^2).$$

Let $1\le k\le d-2$ and let $P^0$ be either $\sigma_d$ or standard Gaussian probability on $\mathbb R^d$. Every full-row-rank $k\times d$ matrix $A$ projects $P^0$ to a Lebesgue density $p_A^0$. With $A^\dagger=A^{\mathsf T}(AA^{\mathsf T})^{-1}$, the spherical density is $$\label{eq:spherical-projection-density}
 p_A^0(y)=\frac{\Gamma(d/2)}{\pi^{k/2}\Gamma((d-k)/2)}
 \frac{(1-\lVert A^\dagger y\rVert^2)^{(d-k-2)/2}}
      {\sqrt{\det(AA^{\mathsf T})}}
 \mathbf 1_{\{\lVert A^\dagger y\rVert<1\}}.$$ For $\lVert A^\dagger y\rVert<1$, its conditional law $P^0_{A,y}$ is uniform on the sphere centered at $A^\dagger y$, of radius $\sqrt{1-\lVert A^\dagger y\rVert^2}$, in the affine space parallel to $\ker A$. For the Gaussian base, $p_A^0$ is the density of $N(0,AA^{\mathsf T})$, and the conditional point is $A^\dagger y$ plus a standard Gaussian in $\ker A$. These conditional laws have jointly measurable versions; unused fibers may be assigned any fixed probability law.*


*If $m\ge1$, $t=m+1$, and $k+m\le d-2$, then $J>0$ almost surely under $(P^0)^{\otimes t}$ and $$\label{eq:inverse-affine-volume}
 \int J(\mathbf u)^{-k}\,(P^0)^{\otimes t}(d\mathbf u)<\infty.$$ If $k+m\le d-3$, the same assertion holds with exponent $k+1$.

Under the hypotheses of Theorem <a href="#thm:equal-label-measure" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:equal-label-measure">15</a>, suppose $p=fP^0$ is a probability. Draw $A\sim\gamma_{k,d}$ independently of $S\sim p$, put $Y=AS$, and conditionally on $(A,Y)$ draw $t$ independent replicas from $p_{A,Y}$. Let $Q$ be the law of their tuple together with $A$, and let $R=p^{\otimes t}(d\mathbf u)\gamma_{k,d}^{F_{\mathbf u}}(dA)$. Then $R$ is a probability and $$\label{eq:replica-likelihood}
 \frac{dQ}{dR}(\mathbf u,A)
 =(2\pi)^{-km/2}J(\mathbf u)^{-k}p_A(Au_1)^{-m}.$$ In particular, the tuple marginal of $Q$ is absolutely continuous relative to $p^{\otimes t}$.

Let $G$ be an $\ell\times m$ standard Gaussian matrix and set $$Z_{k,m}=\mathbb E\det(G^{\mathsf T}G)^{-k/2}.$$ This constant is finite and positive. The nonnegative function $$\label{eq:gaussian-projected-test}
 \Lambda(B,U_1,\ldots,U_t)
 =\frac{c}{Z_{k,m}}
  \det((BD)^{\mathsf T}(BD))^{-k/2}
  p_{C_0B}(C_0U_1)^{-m},$$ with value zero on the exceptional null sets, has expectation one under the law that draws $B$ and projects independent $p$-distributed signals. Conditional on the replica tuple and its original matrix $A$, $\widetilde A$ has law $\gamma_{k,d}^{F}$; this law depends only on the tuple. If $K'$ is the projected total correlation of the replicas, then $$\label{eq:gaussian-correlation-loss}
 K-K'\le Ckm+
 m\mathbb E\!\left[\log p_{\widetilde A}(\widetilde A S_1)-\log p_A(Y)\right].$$ Every logarithm in this display is absolutely integrable.

In the replica experiment with the independent side matrix and $\widetilde A=C_0B$ defined above, $$\label{eq:gaussian-density-comparison}
 \mathbb E\!\left[\log p_{\widetilde A}(\widetilde A S_1)-\log p_A(Y)\right]
 \le Ck+C\log(2+\log L).$$

Suppose $f$ is a probability density supported in the unit ball of $\mathbb R^k$ and $f\le K\alpha_{d,k}$. Then $$\label{haar:maximal-log}
 \int f(u)\log\frac{\mathcal M f(u)}{f(u)}\,\mathrm du
 \le Cd+\log(1+Cd+\log K).$$ Both logarithms are integrable under $f(u)\mathrm du$. The integrand is taken as zero on $\{f=0\}$.

Let $S\sim\sigma$, let $V_f$ be finite-valued, and let the unit-valued output $\widehat S$ be generated from $V_f$ by a measurable kernel using fresh randomness, conditionally independently of $S$. Let $B$ be a Haar $\ell=\lfloor d/4\rfloor$ frame independent of $(S,V_f,\widehat S)$. If $\mathbb P\{\arccos\langle\widehat S,S\rangle\le\epsilon\}\ge2/3$ for $0<\epsilon\le1/10$, then $$\label{haar:terminal-information}
 I(S;V_f\mid B,BS)
 \ge\frac{d-\ell-1}{3}\log\frac1{4\epsilon}-\log2.$$

Let $P=f\sigma_d$ be a probability, where $f$ is a nonnegative Borel function satisfying $f\le\Lambda<\infty$, with $\Lambda\ge1$. Draw $X\sim P$ and $A\sim\gamma_{k,d}$ independently. Let $z=z(A,AX)$ be a measurable function taking at most $K\ge1$ values. Independently of this entire experiment draw $B\sim\gamma_{m,d}$, and put $Y=BX$. With the parameters in <a href="#syn:parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="syn:parameters">[syn:parameters]</a>, $$\label{syn:block-bound}
 I(X;z\mid B,Y)
 \le \frac{\log K}{\ell}
       +C\bigl[d+\log(1+\log\Lambda)\bigr],$$ where $C$ is absolute and independent of $P,\Lambda,K$, and the message rule.

Let $P=f\sigma_d$ be as in Proposition <a href="#syn:block" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="syn:block">32</a>. Fix a tuple $\mathbf x\in(S^{d-1})^\ell$ of affine rank $u$, and put $$x=x_1,\qquad L=L_{\mathbf x},\qquad
 \delta(s)=\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(s-x,L).$$ Draw $B\sim\gamma_{m,d}$. Given $B$, draw $F\sim\gamma_{k,m}^{BL}$, so each row of $F$ is a standard Gaussian in $(BL)^\perp$. Then $$\label{syn:synthetic-bound}
 \mathbb E_{B,F}g_{FB}(FBx)
 \le (2\pi)^{-k/2}a_q\int\delta(s)^{-k}\,P(ds),\qquad
 a_q=\mathbb E\det(HH^{\mathsf T})^{-1/2}\le(q-k-1)^{-k/2},$$ where $H$ is a standard Gaussian $k\times q$ matrix.*






*There is an absolute $C_0>0$ such that the following holds with $$\label{syn:cutoff}
 r_*=\exp\!\left[-\frac{C_0(d+\log\Lambda)}{d-1-u-k}\right]\in(0,1).$$ For every full-row-rank $k\times d$ matrix $A$, put $a=\lVert A\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}$ and define $$\begin{aligned}
 U_A(b)={}&(2\pi)^{-k/2}\mathbf 1_{\{\lVert b\rVert\le a\}}
 \left[1+a^k\int g_A(v)\mathbf 1_{\{\lVert v-b\rVert\le2a\}}\right. \notag\\[-2mm]
 &\hspace{42mm}\left.
       {}\cdot\min\{(ar_*)^{-k},\lVert v-b\rVert^{-k}\}\,dv\right].
 \label{syn:majorant}
\end{aligned}$$ At $v=b$, the second entry in the minimum is interpreted as $+\infty$. If $Ax=b$ and $L\subset\ker A$, then $$\label{syn:kernel-bound}
 (2\pi)^{-k/2}\int\delta(s)^{-k}\,P(ds)\le U_A(b).$$ Writing $V_k$ for the volume of the Euclidean unit ball in $\mathbb R^k$, the same function satisfies $$\begin{aligned}
 \int U_A(b)\,db
 &\le (2\pi)^{-k/2}V_k a^k[2+k\log(2/r_*)],
 \label{syn:majorant-mass}\\
 \mathbb E_{A\sim\gamma_{k,d}}\log\int U_A(b)\,db
 &\le C\bigl[d+\log(1+\log\Lambda)\bigr].
 \label{syn:majorant-log}
\end{aligned}$$ The logarithm in the last display is absolutely integrable, and $\mathbb E_A\log(1+\int U_A)<\infty$.

Let $\mu=f\sigma$ be a probability measure with a Borel density $0\le f\le F<\infty$, where $F\ge1$. Draw $S\sim\mu$ and standard Gaussian matrices $A\in\mathbb R^{n\times d}$ and $B\in\mathbb R^{p\times d}$, all independently. For an integer $w\ge1$, a message $J$ takes values in a fixed $w$-point alphabet (unused symbols are allowed) and is drawn by an arbitrary measurable probability kernel from $(A,AS)$; conditional on $(A,AS)$, its randomness is independent of $(S,B)$. There is an absolute constant $C$ such that $$\label{inc:block-bound}
 I_\mu(S;J\mid B,BS)
 \le \frac{\log w}{k}+Cd+C\log(1+\log F).$$

Put $Z_i=Bs_i$. After $A,C$ are discarded under $R$, $$\label{inc:product-law}
 \mathcal L_R(\mathbf s\mid B,Z_1,\ldots,Z_k,J)
 =\bigotimes_{i=1}^k\mu_{B,Z_i}.$$ Consequently, $$\label{inc:entropy-lower}
 \operatorname{KL}(Q\|R)\ge kI_\mu(S;J\mid B,BS).$$

Suppose $L\in\mathbb R^{q\times d}$ has full row rank and $1\le q\le d-2$. In the interior $$E_L=\{t:t^{\mathsf T}(LL^{\mathsf T})^{-1}t<1\}$$ define a finite measure on $\{s\in\mathbb S^{d-1}:Ls=t\}$ by $$\label{inc:fiber-measure}
 \lambda_{L,t}(ds)=
 \frac{f(s)}{|\mathbb S^{d-1}|\,j_L(s)}
 \,d\operatorname{vol}_{d-1-q}(s),\qquad
 j_L(s)=\det\!\bigl(L(I-ss^{\mathsf T})L^{\mathsf T}\bigr)^{1/2}.$$ Set $\lambda_{L,t}=0$ outside $E_L$. Its mass and normalization are exactly the versions recalled in <a href="#inc:recalled-fibers" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="inc:recalled-fibers">[inc:recalled-fibers]</a>: $$p_L(t)=\lambda_{L,t}(\mathbb S^{d-1}),\qquad
 \mu_{L,t}=\lambda_{L,t}/p_L(t)\quad\text{where }p_L(t)>0.$$ The finite-measure kernel is jointly measurable in $(L,t)$. Moreover, $$\label{inc:ellipsoid-bound}
 V_L\|p_L\|_\infty\le Fd^d,\qquad
 V_L=|E_L|=v_q\det(LL^{\mathsf T})^{1/2}.$$

Let $X,Z$ be second-countable Riemannian manifolds, and let $G:X\times Z\to\mathbb R^a$ be smooth. On a relatively open subset $\Omega$ of $G^{-1}(0)$, suppose both partial differentials $d_xG$ and $d_zG$ are onto. Write $J_xG,J_zG$ for their normal Jacobians. For every nonnegative measurable $h$ on $\Omega$, $$\begin{aligned}
 &\int_Z\int_{\{x:(x,z)\in\Omega\}}
       \frac{h(x,z)}{J_xG(x,z)}
       \,d\operatorname{vol}_{\dim X-a}(x)\,d\operatorname{vol}_Z(z)
       \notag\\
 &\qquad=
 \int_X\int_{\{z:(x,z)\in\Omega\}}
       \frac{h(x,z)}{J_zG(x,z)}
       \,d\operatorname{vol}_{\dim Z-a}(z)\,d\operatorname{vol}_X(x).
 \label{inc:swap-identity}
\end{aligned}$$ The fiber volumes are induced volumes, and the equality holds for nonnegative integrals even when their common value is infinite.

Define the following marginal probability measures of $\mathsf H$: $$\begin{split}
 \mathsf H_A(d\mathbf s,dA)
   &=\mu^{\otimes k}(d\mathbf s)\,\gamma_U(dA),\\
 \mathsf H_C(d\mathbf s,dB,dC)
   &=\mu^{\otimes k}(d\mathbf s)\,\gamma_{p,d}(dB)\,\upsilon_{BU}(dC).
 \end{split}$$ Using the unnormalized measures of <a href="#inc:fiber-measure" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="inc:fiber-measure">[inc:fiber-measure]</a>, define nonnegative measures on the corresponding exact incidence sets by $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathsf M_A(d\mathbf s,dA)
  &=\mu(ds_1)\gamma_{n,d}(dA)
       \prod_{i=2}^k\lambda_{A,As_1}(ds_i),\label{inc:MA}\\
 \mathsf M_C(d\mathbf s,dB,dC)
  &=\mu(ds_1)\gamma_{p,d}(dB)\upsilon_{n,p}(dC)
       \prod_{i=2}^k\lambda_{D,Ds_1}(ds_i),\qquad D=CB.\label{inc:MC}
\end{aligned}$$ Values at row-rank failures may be set to zero. The weights on the right below are assigned arbitrary finite values on their Gram-rank null sets. These measures satisfy the exact identities $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathsf M_A
  &=(2\pi)^{-n\ell/2}\det(U^{\mathsf T}U)^{-n/2}\,\mathsf H_A,
       \label{inc:undivided-A}\\
 \mathsf M_C
  &=\kappa\det(U^{\mathsf T}B^{\mathsf T}BU)^{-n/2}\,\mathsf H_C,
 \qquad
 \kappa=\frac{|\mathcal V_{n,p-\ell}|}{|\mathcal V_{n,p}|}.
       \label{inc:undivided-C}
\end{aligned}$$ These are equalities of nonnegative measures.*



*For $L=A,D$, the values $p_L(Ls_1)$ are positive and finite $\mathsf H$-almost surely and equal the shrinking-ball limits $$p_L(Ls_1)=\lim_{j\to\infty}
 \frac{\mu\{z:\|Lz-Ls_1\|<2^{-j}\}}{v_n2^{-jn}}.$$ On this full-measure set, the densities of $Q$ and $R$ with respect to $\mathsf H$ are $$\begin{aligned}
 q_*&=w\phi_J(A,As_1)(2\pi)^{-n\ell/2}
       \det(U^{\mathsf T}U)^{-n/2}p_A(As_1)^{-\ell},
       \label{inc:q-density}\\
 r_*&=\kappa\det(U^{\mathsf T}B^{\mathsf T}BU)^{-n/2}
       p_D(Ds_1)^{-\ell}.\label{inc:r-density}
\end{aligned}$$ In particular $Q\ll R$.

For $1\le n\le h$, with the induced Euclidean matrix metric, $$\label{inc:stiefel-volume}
 |\mathcal V_{n,h}|=
 2^{n(n-1)/4}\prod_{j=1}^n|\mathbb S^{h-j}|.$$ Consequently, for <a href="#inc:dimensions" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="inc:dimensions">[inc:dimensions]</a>, $$\label{inc:kappa}
 \kappa=\pi^{-n\ell/2}\prod_{j=1}^n
 \frac{\Gamma((p-j+1)/2)}{\Gamma((p-j+1-\ell)/2)}
 \ge\left(\frac{p-n+1-\ell}{2\pi}\right)^{n\ell/2}.$$

Under $Q$, define $$\begin{split}
 K&=\int
   \operatorname{dist}(z-s_1,\operatorname{col}U)^{-n}\,d\mu(z),\\
 p_A^*(t)&=\sup_{\rho>0}
   \frac{\mu\{z:\|Az-t\|<\rho\}}{v_n\rho^n},\qquad
 K_0=v_n\|A\|_{\mathrm{op}}^n p_A^*(As_1).
 \end{split}$$ Then $K_0\ge2^{-n}$, and $$\label{inc:kernel-bound}
 K\le K_0L_F,\qquad
 L_F=1+\frac{n}{d-\ell-n}+n\log(2/\tau),\qquad
 \tau=\left(\frac{2^{-n}}{Fd^d}\right)^{1/(d-\ell-n)}.$$ In particular, $\log L_F\le C\log d+C\log(1+\log F)$, and $K<\infty$. Moreover, $$\label{inc:conditional-density}
 \mathbb E_Q[p_D(Ds_1)\mid\mathbf s,A,y,J]
 \le (2\pi)^{-n/2}K .$$

For every full-rank $A$, $$\label{inc:maximal-bound}
 \int\log\frac{p_A^*(As)}{p_A(As)}\,d\mu(s)
 \le Cd+C\log(1+\log F).$$ The integrand is nonnegative almost surely.

There is a universal $c>0$ with the following property. Let $M=M(d)$ be a nonnegative integer with $M(d)=o(d^2)$. For all sufficiently large $d$, with the threshold allowed to depend on the rate $M(d)/d^2\to0$, suppose $S\sim\sigma$ and a learner in Definition <a href="#def:streaming-model" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="def:streaming-model">3</a> stops by a deterministic integer horizon $T\ge0$. If $$\Pr\{\angle(\widehat S,S)\le\epsilon\}\ge2/3,
 \qquad 0<\epsilon\le1/10,$$ then $$\label{inc:precision-bound}
 T\ge c\,d\log(1/\epsilon).$$ The bound permits arbitrary computation within a transition.

Put $L_\epsilon=\log(1/\epsilon)$. Pad stopping to $T$ by retaining the stopped state and its stopping index. At most $$\label{inc:width}
 w=(T+2)2^M$$ padded states suffice. Under the hypotheses of Theorem <a href="#inc:precision" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="inc:precision">44</a>, if $T<dL_\epsilon$, then $$\label{inc:short-bounds}
 L_\epsilon=O(1+M/d)=o(d),\qquad
 T=O(d^2),\qquad \log w=o(d^2).$$
